WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.368
"What’s the Ideal BMI and Waist Size?"

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.140
We seem to have become inured
to the mortal threat of obesity.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.780
If you go back in the medical
literature a half century or so

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.480
when obesity wasn’t
just run-of-the-mill,

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.060
the descriptions are
much more grim:

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:29.280
“Obesity is always tragic, and
its hazards are terrifying.”

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.780
But it’s not just obesity.
Of the 4 million deaths every year

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.720
attributed to excess body fat,
nearly 40% of the victims

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.600
are just overweight, not obese.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.600
According to two famous
Harvard studies, weight gain

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.320
of as little as 11 pounds from
early adulthood through middle age

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.660
increases the risk of
major chronic diseases,

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.920
such as diabetes, cardiovascular
disease, and cancer.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.760
The flipside, though, is
that even modest weight loss

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.160
can have major health benefits.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.620
What’s the optimal BMI?

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.560
The largest studies in the
United States and around the world

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.580
found that having a normal body
mass index, a BMI from 20 to 25,

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.560
is associated with
the longest lifespan.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.800
Put all the best available
studies with the longest

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.640
follow-up together and that
can be narrowed down even further

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.980
to a BMI of 20 to 22.
That would be about

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.240
between 124 to 136 pounds for
someone who stands five-foot-six.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.280
You can pause the video
here to use this unisex chart

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.480
to see what your optimal weight
might be based on your height.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.520
But, even within a normal BMI,
the risk of developing

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.340
chronic diseases, such as
type 2 diabetes, heart disease,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.180
and several types of cancer starts
to rise towards the upper end,

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.000
even starting as low as a BMI of 21.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.760
A BMI of 18.5 and 24.5 are both
considered within the normal range,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.060
but a BMI of 24.5 may
be associated with twice

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.800
the heart disease risk compared 18.5.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.480
Look at this diabetes graph
among women, a five-fold difference

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.520
in diabetes rates all within
the so-called ideal range under 25.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.140
Just as there are gradations
of risk within a normal BMI range,

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.480
there is a spectrum within obesity.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.900
Class III obesity, a BMI
over 40, can be associated

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.980
with the loss of a decade of life or more.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.820
At a BMI greater than 45,
such as a five-foot-six person

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.880
at 280 pounds, life
expectancy may shrink

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.580
to that of a cigarette smoker.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.500
There are, however,
so-called “obesity skeptics”

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.560
that argue that the health
consequences of obesity are unclear

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.060
or even greatly exaggerated.
They are a motley bunch,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:51.000
ranging from feminists,
queer theorists, and new ageists

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.180
to “far right wing, pro-gun,
pro-America websites

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.360
where the idea [is] that
obesity alarmists are nanny-state

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.200
communists who simply want
to stop us from having fun….”

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.640
Unlike activists who,
for example, organized to raise

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.040
consciousness and stamp
out the AIDS epidemic,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.180
the size acceptance movement
appears to have the opposite goal,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.080
rallying for less public awareness
and treatment of the problem.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.180
(They do have good slogans though:

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.360
“We’re here, we’re spheres,
get used to it!”)

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.320
I’m all for fighting size
stigma and discrimination—

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.080
I have a whole section on
weight stigma in my new book—

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.320
but the adverse health
consequences of obesity

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.800
are an established scientific fact.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.520
Can’t you be fat but fit?

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.980
In a study of more
than 600 centenarians

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.040
(those living over 100)
only about 1% of the women

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.220
and not a single one
of the men were obese.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.760
But there does appear to be a
rare subgroup of obese individuals

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.400
who don’t suffer the
typical metabolic costs

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.200
such as high blood
pressure and cholesterol.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.920
This raises the possibility that
there may be such thing as

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.800
“benign obesity” or “healthy obesity.”

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.620
It may just be a matter of time, though,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.240
before the risk factors develop.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.520
And even if they don’t,
followed long enough,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.240
even quote-unquote “metabolically
healthy” obese individuals

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.340
are at increased risk of
diabetes, and fatty liver disease

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.280
and cardiovascular
events such as heart attacks,

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:21.840
and/or premature death.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.660
Bottom line: there
is strong evidence

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.440
that so-called “healthy
obesity” is a myth.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.520
Many “fat-activists” try to
downplay the risks of obesity,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.060
even as they may be among
the epidemic’s greatest victims.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.900
Lynn McAfee is the
director of medical advocacy

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.660
for the Council on Size
and Weight Discrimination

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.720
and routinely takes
part in obesity conferences

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.280
and government panels on obesity.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.940
“I’m not actually particularly
that interested in [health],”

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.741
she is quoted as saying,
“and God I hate science.”

00:04:57.074 --> 00:05:01.020
There was a book originally published in
the '80s, and then repeatedly republished,

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.660
entitled "DIETING MAKES YOU FAT".

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.320
Since most people who lose
weight go on to regain it,

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.800
the concern is that there may
be adverse health consequences

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.320
of so-called yo-yo dieting.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.600
This idea emerged from
animal studies that showed,

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:20.220
for example, detrimental effects of
starving and refeeding obese rats.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:24.840
This captured the media's attention,
leading to a pervasive common belief

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.400
about the dangers of weight cycling,
discouraging people from even trying.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.420
Even the animal data is
inconclusive though.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:36.900
For example, weight cycling
mice makes them live longer.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.860
Most importantly though, a review
of the human data concluded

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.800
that evidence for an adverse effect
of weight cycling appears sparse,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.240
if it exists at all.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.860
Bottom line:
Yo-Yo Dieting is Better Than None.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:55.560
Ideally, we'd get down to a BMI of 20
to 22, but body mass index doesn't

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.380
take the composition of the
weight into account. For example,

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.820
bodybuilders are heavy for their
height, but can be extremely lean.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:06.660
The gold standard measure of
obesity is percentage body fat,

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.980
but an accurate calculation can
be complicated and expensive.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.860
All you need to measure
BMI is height and weight,

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.060
but it may underestimate
the true prevalence of obesity.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.580
The World Health
Organization defines obesity

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:26.580
as a body fat percentage over
25% in men or 35% in women.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.360
At a BMI of 25, which is
considered just barely overweight,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:35.100
body fat percentages in a representative
US sample of adults varied between

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:39.960
14% and 35% in men, and
26% and 43% in women.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.100
So you could be normal
weight but actually obese.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:49.080
Using the BMI cutoff for obesity only
about 1 in 5 Americans were obese

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:52.680
back in the 90's, but based on
their body fat, the true proportion

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.260
even back then was closer to 50%.

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:59.340
Half of America is not just
overweight, but obese.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:04.140
So just using BMI, doctors may
misclassify more than half

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:08.280
of obese individuals as being just
overweight or even normal weight

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.460
and miss an opportunity to intervene.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.600
The important thing is not the label,
though, but the health consequences.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.620
Ironically, BMI appears to
be an even better predictor

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.860
of cardiovascular disease death
than percentage body fat.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.420
I mean, that suggests that excess
weight from any source, fat or lean,

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:30.000
may not be healthy in the long run.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.380
The lifespan of bodybuilders
does seem to be cut short.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.680
They have about a third higher mortality
rate than the general population.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:45.780
The average age of death was around
48 years old, but this may well be due

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.620
in part to the toxic effects
of anabolic steroids on the heart.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:54.780
Pre-eminent nutritional physiologist Ancel
Keys (after which K-rations were named)

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:57.240
suggested the mirror method.

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.060
If you really want to know
whether you are obese,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.460
just undress and look
at yourself in the mirror.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.940
Don't worry about our fancy
laboratory measurements; you'll know!

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.760
All fat is not the same, though.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.580
There is the pinchable superficial flab

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.680
that you may see
jiggling about your body,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.720
but then there's the riskier, deeper
visceral fat which coils around

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.880
and infiltrates
your internal organs.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.740
Measuring BMI is simple, cheap, and
effective, but does not take into account

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.380
the distribution of fat on the body,

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.020
whereas waist circumference
can provide a measure

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:33.600
of the deep underlying belly fat.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:37.440
Both BMI and waist circumference can
be used to predict the risk of death

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.460
due to excess body fat, but even
at the same BMI there appears to

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:47.160
be nearly a straight-line increase in
mortality risk with widening waistline.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.160
Someone with normal-weight
central obesity, meaning someone

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.800
not even overweight according to BMI,

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.180
but fat around the middle may
have up to twice the risk of dying

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.600
compared to even someone who's obese
according to their height and weight.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.660
This is why the current
recommendations recommend

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.740
measuring both: BMI
and waist circumference.

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:11.820
This may be especially important for
older women who lose approximately

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.600
13 pounds of bone and muscle
as they age from 25 to 65,

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.060
while quadrupling their visceral fat
stores (men tend to only double).

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:26.040
So even if a woman doesn't gain any
weight based on the bathroom scale,

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.260
she may be gaining fat.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.020
What's the waistline cut-off?

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.020
Increased risk of
metabolic complications

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:41.640
start at an abdominal circumference
of 31.5" in women, and 37" in most men,

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:47.520
though closer to 35.5" for South Asian,
Chinese, and Japanese men.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:51.600
The benchmark for substantially
increased risk starts

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.920
at about 34.5" for women,
and 40" for men.

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:01.440
Once you get over an abdominal
circumference of about 43" in men,

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.340
mortality rates shoot up
about 50%, compared to men

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:06.900
with 8-inch-smaller stomachs.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:13.380
And women suffer 80% greater mortality
risk at 37.5" compared to 27.5".

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:19.860
The reading of a measuring tape may
translate into years off one's lifespan.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:25.620
The good news is the riskiest
fat is the easiest to lose.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.980
Your body appears smart
enough to preferentially shed

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:31.200
the villainous visceral fat first.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.360
Although it may take losing

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:35.640
as much as 20% of your
weight to realize significant

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.040
improvements in quality
of life for most individuals

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:42.420
with severe obesity, your disease
risk drops almost immediately.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:47.040
At 3% weight loss (just like 6 pounds
for someone weighing 200 pounds)

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.160
your blood sugar control and
triglycerides start to get better.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.220
At 5%, your blood pressure
and cholesterol improve.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:57.780
Just a 5% weight loss (about 10
pounds for someone starting at 200)

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:02.665
may cut your risk of
developing diabetes in half.

